WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.617
You install Homebrew, the installer for almost everything else in this course.

2
00:00:07.617 --> 00:00:08.815
Then you type brew.

3
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:11.493
And the shell says: command not found.

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.296
Homebrew is on your disk.

5
00:00:13.296 --> 00:00:15.276
So why can't the shell find it?

6
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.784
Your shell is the program that runs what you type.

7
00:00:18.784 --> 00:00:22.015
When you type a name, it does not search the whole disk.

8
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:27.360
It looks in each folder on a list called your PATH, in order, and it runs the first match.

9
00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:29.256
Watch it look for brew.

10
00:00:29.256 --> 00:00:31.018
Not in the first folder.

11
00:00:31.018 --> 00:00:32.193
Not in the next.

12
00:00:32.193 --> 00:00:33.588
Not in any of them.

13
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.156
The list ends, so the shell gives up: command not found.

14
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:46.399
On Apple Silicon Macs, Homebrew lives in /opt/homebrew, and puts its commands in /opt/homebrew/bin.

15
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.541
But the PATH that macOS starts with does not include that folder.

16
00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:53.269
The tool is in the room, but not on the list.

17
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.820
When the installer finishes, it prints Next steps: two or three commands.

18
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.193
Most people get stuck right here, because they skip them.

19
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.164
Run exactly what it prints.

20
00:01:03.164 --> 00:01:06.235
Those lines add Homebrew's folder to your PATH.

21
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:12.921
They also save the change in ~/.zprofile, a file each new shell reads when it starts.

22
00:01:12.921 --> 00:01:14.971
So open a new terminal tab.

23
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.981
Now type brew again.

24
00:01:16.981 --> 00:01:21.124
The search reaches Homebrew's folder, finds brew, and stops.

25
00:01:21.124 --> 00:01:22.575
The first match wins.

26
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.781
First match wins matters for Python too.

27
00:01:25.781 --> 00:01:29.976
Homebrew's default Python is newer than the one this course uses.

28
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:38.569
So you install python@3.14, and type python3.14 when you create the project's virtual environment.

29
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.444
A venv is a private folder of packages for one project.

30
00:01:42.444 --> 00:01:46.088
It has its own bin folder, with its own python and pip.

31
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.478
Activating it puts .venv/bin first on your PATH.

32
00:01:49.478 --> 00:01:52.457
Your prompt starts with dot venv in brackets.

33
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:55.866
Now python and pip mean the copies inside the venv.

34
00:01:55.866 --> 00:01:58.501
The search stops at the very first folder.

35
00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:02.265
So this project's Flask cannot clash with another project's.

36
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:04.921
deactivate puts your PATH back.

37
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:09.438
Forget to activate, and pip is found in Homebrew's folder instead.

38
00:02:09.438 --> 00:02:13.808
Homebrew's own Python refuses, with externally-managed-environment.

39
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.347
That error means one thing: your venv is not active.

40
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:21.117
When the wrong version of a tool runs, try which -a.

41
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:25.297
It lists every match on your PATH, in the order the shell tries them.

42
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.782
So the shell runs the first match on your PATH.

43
00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:33.380
Homebrew's Next steps add its folder, and a venv puts its own folder first.
